            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A physical reminder to remember and obey God's commandments, representing holiness, covenant loyalty, and devotion to God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Numbers 15:37-41\",\"Deuteronomy 22:12\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A conduit or point of contact for divine healing and faith, often associated with the 'wings' of the Messiah's garment.\",\"basis\":[\"Malachi 4:2\",\"Matthew 9:20\",\"Mark 5:27\",\"Luke 8:44\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Outward religious ostentation, legalism, or hypocrisy when enlarged for public display.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 23:5\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The royal and priestly status of the believer, symbolized specifically by the inclusion of the blue thread (tekhelet).\",\"basis\":[\"Numbers 15:38\",\"Exodus 28:31\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Christian interpretation universally recognizes the Old Testament definition of the tzitzit as a mnemonic for the Law. New Testament interpretations frequently emphasize Christology (Jesus wearing the tzitzit and fulfilling the Law) and the contrast between inner faith (the woman with the issue of blood reaching for the hem) and outward show (the Pharisees enlarging their tassels).\"}",
